      "text": "To find the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to understand the periodicity of the individual trigonometric functions involved, which are \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\).\n\n1. **Periods of Trigonometric Functions:**\n   - The function \\(\\tan x\\) has a period of \\(\\pi\\). This can be shown by noting that \\(\\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x\\) for all \\(x\\).\n   - Similarly, \\(\\cot x\\) also has a period of \\(\\pi\\). This can be shown by noting that \\(\\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\\) for all \\(x\\).\n\n2. **Analyzing the Function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\):**\n   - Since \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) both have a period of \\(\\pi\\), any transformation involving \\(\\tan x\\) or \\(\\cot x\\) will maintain this period.\n   - When we add these two functions, the period of the new function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) will also be determined by the least common multiple (LCM) of their individual periods.\n\n3. **Finding the Least Common Multiple (LCM):**\n   - The periods 1 (for \\(\\tan x\\)) and 1 (for \\(\\cot x\\)) are the same, so their LCM is 1.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - Since the periods of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) are both 1, the period of the sum \\(\\tan x + \\cot x\\) is also 1.\n\nTherefore, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
